(i) Constitutionalism: the principle that the powers of government are limited by a constitution and that government must act according to the constitution and the rule of law. Features:
• supremacy of the constitution (e.g. section 1 of Nigeria's 1999 Constitution);
• limited government;
• protection of fundamental human rights (Chapter IV);
• rule of law;
• independent judiciary;
• periodic free elections;
• separation of powers.
Example: courts can declare actions of government unconstitutional and void. Constitutionalism prevents tyranny and arbitrary rule. It is undermined by military rule and the suspension of constitutions.

(ii) Separation of powers: the principle, associated with Baron de Montesquieu (The Spirit of the Laws, 1748), that the three functions of government (legislative, executive and judicial) should be performed by three separate organs with different personnel, so that power is not concentrated in one person or body.
• The legislature (e.g. the National Assembly) makes laws.
• The executive (the President and ministers) implements laws.
• The judiciary (the courts) interprets laws.
Example: the USA and Nigeria's presidential system, where ministers cannot be members of the legislature. Purpose: to prevent tyranny and protect liberty. In practice, separation is not absolute (in parliamentary systems such as Britain, the executive is drawn from the legislature).

(iii) Checks and balances: a system in which each organ of government can control or limit the powers of the others, so that no organ becomes too powerful. Examples in Nigeria and the USA:
• the President can veto bills passed by the legislature, and the legislature can override the veto by a two-thirds majority;
• the Senate confirms the President's appointments (ministers, ambassadors, judges);
• the legislature can impeach the President;
• the legislature controls the budget;
• the courts can declare laws or executive actions unconstitutional (judicial review);
• the President appoints judges on the advice of the National Judicial Council, with Senate confirmation.
It complements separation of powers and promotes accountability.
